app jws-spec [
  version: "1.0.0",
  description: "Design contract for verifying a compact JWS signed with RS256 (RFC 7515, RFC 7518 section 3.3). JWS_VERIFY { token, jwk { kty, n, e, alg?, kid? } } answers JWS_VERIFIED { header { alg, typ?, kid? }, payload } or JWS_REFUSED { reason }. The token is exactly three non-empty dot-separated base64url segments (no padding, no length-1-mod-4 segment), else malformed; header and payload must decode to JSON objects, else malformed. The header alg must be exactly RS256: none, HS256 and every other value (or an absent alg) refuse alg-not-allowed. jwk.kty must be RSA (key-type); a jwk.alg that is set must equal the header alg (alg-mismatch); when both header and jwk carry a kid they must be equal (kid-mismatch). n, e and the signature are converted from base64url to padded standard base64 and std-rsa verifies the signature over the ASCII text header.payload exactly as received; a false verdict answers signature and a std-rsa refusal keeps its own reason. The payload is returned as a typed record (iss, sub, aud as a string or list, exp, nbf, iat, auth_time, email, name, firebase.tenant; extra holds every claim of the token, nested objects included, so the typed fields are projections of it; an absent claim stays absent) and the claim policy (time, issuer, audience) stays in std-bearer-verify. Golden vectors (openssl 2048-bit, e 65537): the valid token verifies; one changed payload byte, another key's modulus and a flipped signature bit refuse signature; alg none and alg HS256 refuse alg-not-allowed; two segments refuse malformed; header kid k1 against jwk kid k2 refuses kid-mismatch. ES256 (RFC 7518 section 3.4): the header alg is RS256 or ES256, nothing else. With ES256 jwk { kty EC, crv P-256, x, y } (base64url, 32 bytes each) and the signature is the raw 64-byte r||s in base64url, verified by std-ecdsa over the ASCII text header.payload exactly as received. A kty other than EC refuses key-type, a crv other than P-256 refuses curve, a missing x or y refuses key-missing, a jwk.alg that differs from the header alg refuses alg-mismatch, and std-ecdsa refusals keep their own reason. Golden ES256 vectors (openssl prime256v1, key 1 of the ecdsa spec; jwk x=lIDYhkqscUItd6oN2IL11yMr74OBpa10Jmuw1wbtzVE y=1WSlIjIpQ79tukDvI5mpfuEnky4e3zHM2jcMPAfjGHc): token eyJhbGciOiJFUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJodHRwczovL2lzc3Vlci5leGFtcGxlIiwic3ViIjoidXNlci0xIiwiYXVkIjoiYXBwIiwiZXhwIjo0MTAyNDQ0ODAwLCJlbWFpbCI6ImFAYi5jIn0.Dwj8EPznXWGw-J3V5VQn5SyrjpABLKyLP6_JepCgPiHvcpn-uSW0XFSQk9l0esTXCOL5JLGVzjQr7z-0inGLqg verifies; the same signature under a payload with sub user-2 (eyJpc3MiOiJodHRwczovL2lzc3Vlci5leGFtcGxlIiwic3ViIjoidXNlci0yIiwiYXVkIjoiYXBwIiwiZXhwIjo0MTAyNDQ0ODAwLCJlbWFpbCI6ImFAYi5jIn0) refuses signature; the valid token against kty RSA refuses key-type; against crv P-384 refuses curve; against jwk.alg RS256 refuses alg-mismatch; a header alg none refuses alg-not-allowed. RS256 control: the RS256 vectors above are unchanged (valid verifies, a changed payload refuses signature)."
]

type JwsHeader = { alg : string, typ : string, kid : string }
type JwsFirebase = { tenant : string }
type JsonValue = string | number | boolean | [JsonValue] | Map string JsonValue
type JwsExtra = Map string JsonValue
type JwsClaims = { iss : string, sub : string, aud : (string | [string]), exp : number, nbf : number, iat : number, auth_time : number, email : string, name : string, firebase : JwsFirebase, extra : JwsExtra }

orbital JwsSpec {
  expects entity JwsRun [runtime] {
    id : string!
  }
  expects trait JwsVerifier {
    state idle {
      JWS_VERIFY -> idle
    }
    emits {
      JWS_VERIFIED { header : JwsHeader!  payload : JwsClaims! }
      JWS_REFUSED { reason : string! }
    }
  }
  expects page "/jws" -> JwsVerifier
}
